# First Aid Form Upgrade: 2025/26 Season Analysis

**Source:** First Aid Form Data.xlsx (OneDrive inbox, as at 9 August 2026)
**Method:** All figures re-derived from the raw season sheets (20-21 through 25-26_NEW) using the scripts in `analysis/`. The colleague-crunched sheets (Year 2 Year, Stats (25-26), By Unit, Resus) were treated as claims to reconcile, not as sources. Every 2025/26 figure in those sheets that was tested reconciled exactly; the prior-season formulas in Year 2 Year are broken and corrected figures appear below.
**Scope discipline:** Nothing in this document relies on information outside the workbook. Limitations are stated at the end. No narrative text from any record is reproduced.

---

## 1. About the first aid form

Whenever a lifesaver, lifeguard or event first aider treats a person, a first aid form is completed in LSV's incident management system. The record captures who was treated, what they presented with, what was done, and how the patient left care. Cases a crew classifies as major can be linked to an LSV Communications incident number so the first aid record and the operational incident sit together.

Until November 2025 every case saw the same flat form: a fixed set of fields (injury type, initial treatment, body location, activity at the time, who the patient was released to) plus one free-text notes box where all other clinical detail had to be written. Data that did not fit the fixed fields existed only as prose.

The new form presents question sets based on the case. The nature of the presentation (injury, medical, envenomation, environmental, mental health, suspected drowning, resuscitation, drug or alcohol) opens a specific-problem question set for that nature. Treatments given open their own sets: administering oxygen asks for method, time applied and duration; administering methoxyflurane asks for vial number, dose times, contraindications, adverse effects and pain ratings before, at two minutes and at four minutes; performing resuscitation asks for equipment, airway assessment, method, time of first compression, defibrillation time, CPR cycles, first AED rhythm, shock count and the patient's state at handover. The discharge set records the pathway, ambulance handover and the patient's condition, and a closing set asks whether the responder needs peer support or experienced occupational violence. A member sees only the questions that apply to the case in front of them.

The old form was retired on 9 November 2025 and the new form went live on 12 November 2025.

## 2. Executive summary

Six seasons of raw data (7,406 cases) support four conclusions.

1. **The new form measures things the old form structurally could not.** Documentation delay, completion state, clinical timelines for resuscitation, analgesia audit trails, patient outcomes and responder welfare are all visible for the first time.
2. **The branching design works.** When a conditional question set is presented it is answered 96.6 to 100 per cent of the time, and every gap is accounted for (section 7).
3. **Documentation is fast and timely.** Median time from opening a form to its final edit is about 4 minutes; 87.1 per cent of cases are documented within one hour of treatment; 95.0 per cent of forms reached completed status.
4. **The data now describes the work directly.** Presentations, patients, responders, timing, treatments and outcomes are counted from structured fields (section 4), and the same data identifies its own improvement and training targets (sections 6 and 8).

