## 10. Where to next

### 10.1 One action form

The first aid form and the rescue form will be combined into a single action form with one clear definition for members: one form for one person assisted. This removes the need for a member to remember to complete one or both forms, to decide which form applies, or to enter the same personal details twice. It will be one form, with questions presented dynamically for the rescue, for the first aid, or for both.

The result is a record that is accurately captured, reported cohesively, and linked to incidents consistently. The 48 records in this season's data where the major classification and the incident link disagree, and the suspected drowning with no incident number, are the kind of inconsistency the single form is designed to remove at source. Above all, the organisation gains consistency and completeness of data that allow it to be more informed than at any previous point.

The first aid form project has been a key signifier of what is possible when the system meets the need, and of the benefits of accurate, complete and consistent data.

### 10.2 Incident categorisation

Following this work, a similar program will be applied to the incident categorisation approach. It will simplify categorisation for members, capture more accurately what is being done on the beach, and make the system easier to use and understand, with clear standards on how information is captured. Stronger data quality and consistency will in turn support operational decisions, reporting, funding submissions and everything else that good data enables.

## 11. Limitations

- The workbook contains no user-satisfaction survey; satisfaction claims rest on behavioural evidence (completion rate, speed, adoption, branch answer rates).
- Resuscitation (7) and methoxyflurane (14) counts are small; report them as counts.
- Old and new taxonomies differ; cross-form trend comparisons use shared categories only.
- Timestamps measure documentation behaviour, not care delivery; the completion long tail includes administrative reopens.
- Season-on-season volume comparisons use the matched November-to-June window.
- Unit type is derived from unit names in the data, not from a separate classification.

